## Summary

Most ledger object types are only expected to be modified by a specific set of transaction types. Enforce that.

For example,
* An `Escrow` object should only be created by an `EscrowCreate`, deleted by `EscrowCancel` or `EscrowFinish`, and never modified.
* An `Offer` object should only be created by an `OfferCreate`, modified by a `Payment` or `OfferCreate`, or deleted by a `Payment`, `OfferCancel`, or `OfferCreate`.
* An `AccountRoot` should only be created by a `Payment`, modified by any non-pseudo transaction, and deleted by `AccountDelete`, `AMMWithdraw`, or `AMMDelete`.

This partial list is obviously not exhaustive, and may not be correct. Part of the work will be determining the full transaction list for every object type.

## Motivation

This idea came out of the an online discussion @mDuo13 that was focused on #4646.

## Solution

At a high level,
* For every object type, figure out which transactions are able to create, modify, and delete it.
* In `visitEntry`, build a list of affected object types, and which operation(s) are done to it.
* In `finalize`, iterate over that list, checking that the transaction type is allowed to make that change. The code could look something like:
```
auto const txType = tx.getTxnType();
for (auto const [objectType, operation] : changeList)
{
  switch (objectType)
  {
    case ltESCROW:
      switch (operation)
      {
        case create:
           if(txType != ttESCROW_CREATE)
             return false;
           break;
         case modify:
            return false;
         case delete:
            if (txType != ttESCROW_FINISH || txType != ttESCROW_CANCEL)
             return false;
            break;
      }
      break;
    [...]
  }
}
```
Or it might be better to build a `constexpr` lookup table that specifies all those rules. I don't want to get too deep into implementation details here.
